"Mirrors a density estimate within each group to show distribution shape and optionally a median or quartile summary.\n\nUse when: Group shapes and multiple modes matter beyond compact quartiles. Samples are large enough to justify density estimation.\n\nAvoid when: Tiny groups or heavily discrete values make smooth shapes unreliable.\n\nVisual grammar: Position on the numeric axis encodes the measured value. Violin half-width encodes estimated density under a declared normalization.\n\nBuild: Estimate group densities with a documented common bandwidth policy. Choose equal-area, equal-maximum, or count-aware normalization and name it. Mirror curves around group centers and mark medians or sample sizes separately.\n\nCheck: Verify densities respect plausible bounds or disclose boundary correction. Check the same width interpretation applies to every group. Compare the shape against raw observations or a histogram.\n\nPitfalls: Equal-width violins can conceal very unequal sample sizes. Smoothed tails can extend past observed values unless trimmed or explained.\n\nExample: Compare synthetic wait-time shapes across three queues, showing medians and group counts beside each violin.\n\nAlternatives: Density plot, Raincloud plot, Box plot."
